**Theorem 160.1 (Complete closed six-coordinate equality).** For every
real parameter profile in $Q_6$,

$$
\Pi_N\cap Q_6=P=\Pi_O\cap Q_6.
\tag{160.1}
$$

There is a continuous piecewise affine choice of the full 406-coordinate
stopped allocation on all of $P$, with all word laws and all cap rows
satisfied simultaneously and $p_{1^6}(0)=1-e$.
It includes zero cap, zero amplitudes, all ties, $a_6=e$ and zero schedule
weights. In the coefficient notation of Sections 108--110, it supplies
$\mathcal F_N(a,e)\cap\mathscr C_6\ne\varnothing$ throughout $P$.

**Proof.** A bounded polytope is the convex hull of its vertices.
For this particular finite intersection an elementary argument suffices:
if a point is not a vertex, split it along a chord of its minimal face,
whose endpoints belong to proper faces; induction on face dimension
expresses it as a convex combination of vertices. Take
$x=\sum_v\lambda_vv$, $\lambda_v\ge0$, $\sum_v\lambda_v=1$ and mix
entire endpoint tables by $T(x)=\sum_v\lambda_vT(v)$.
Every mass equation, word law and TV row in $(x,T)$ is affine.
Their nonnegativity and inequalities therefore survive this single
mixture. The all-positive base identity becomes
$\sum_v\lambda_v(1-e_v)=1-e$. Thus $P\subseteq\Pi_O$.
Completion gives $\Pi_O\subseteq\Pi_N$, and Proposition 157.2 gives the
other inclusion in (160.1). Theorem 109.1 identifies the stopped kernel
with $\mathscr C_6$. This is a convex-hull proof for arbitrary real
parameters; no rational approximation or limiting acquisition is used.

For the continuous choice use the pulling triangulation with the global
vertex order of Theorem 158.2. Recursively on each face, choose its least
vertex and cone it over the already triangulated facets that do not
contain that vertex. Induction on face dimension proves both coverage
and compatibility of the induced triangulations on shared faces: the
same ordering is used on every face. Equivalently, intersecting cones
have their common subdivided boundary faces. Thus this finite collection
is a triangulation of $P$ whose vertices are the original vertices.
Assign each vertex its one table from Definition 159.1. On a simplex
interpolate those tables by its barycentric coordinates. On an
intersection, coordinates of vertices outside the common face vanish,
and the remaining affine coordinates coincide. The tables agree there,
so the finite piecewise affine section is continuous. Feasibility and
the base identity follow from the preceding affine argument.

Here is a joint measurable one-seed realization, including zero schedule
masses. Order schedules and labels as above. Let
$q_S(x)=\sum_{S'<S}w_{S'}(x)$ and use the half-open schedule interval
$[q_S,q_S+w_S)\subset[0,1)$. In each counterfactual cell $c$, emit $r$
when

$$
q_S+\sum_{r'<r}T_{S,c,r'}\le U
 <q_S+\sum_{r'\le r}T_{S,c,r'}.
\tag{160.2}
$$

Every cell partitions the same schedule interval. Its absolute cutpoints
are continuous in $x$; the events are jointly Borel. Empty intervals
select nothing, so no division by zero is required. Before acquisition
$U$ fixes the schedule and its entire table. Execute that schedule until
its first queried zero or exhaustion and output its selected label.
There are at most two actual requests. Completing it in $N$ uses the
same $U$ and counts every remaining request. The allocation is continuous;
individual emitted labels need not be continuous in $x$ or $U$.
This section is on $P$ only. $\square$

